        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Leipzig</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">16 to 19 October 1813</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, between the Pleiße and the Parthe rivers</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Sixth Coalition (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>), led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a>, Karl von <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-philipp-prince-of-schwarzenberg">Schwarzenberg</a>, Gebhard von <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gebhard-leberecht-von-blucher">Blücher</a> and Crown Prince Charles John of Sweden, with around 380,000 troops and 1,500 guns; the French under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, with around 198,000 troops, or up to 225,000 by other estimates, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, Italians and Germans of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Coalition decisively defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, who was compelled to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Estimates of the total killed, wounded or missing range from 80,000 to 110,000.</span></div>
