    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1812</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> invades <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> with around 685,000 troops and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grande-armee">Grande Armée</a> is virtually destroyed in the retreat</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">May 1813</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> wins at Lützen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bautzen">Bautzen</a>, and a brief armistice follows</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 1813</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> joins the Coalition and the Trachenberg Plan is set, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> wins at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> on 27 August</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September 1813</span><span class="rf-text">Oudinot is beaten at Großbeeren and Ney at Dennewitz, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> withdraws westward to organise his forces around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 1813</span><span class="rf-text">The fighting at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> begins on 16 October on the southern and northern fronts and ends in a draw</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 1813</span><span class="rf-text">On 18 October the Coalition encircles <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s army and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> troops defect, and that night he begins the retreat</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 1813</span><span class="rf-text">On 19 October the Elster bridge is blown while the French cross it, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> falls to the Coalition</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1814</span><span class="rf-text">The Coalition invades <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> falls on 31 March, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> abdicates on 6 April</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-leipzig" data-v="7882532096" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The French round Leipzig, the Coalition armies closing</b> Napoleon drew his army round Leipzig, with Ney and Marmont on the north, MacDonald on the east, Poniatowski and Augereau on the south-east and Bertrand's corps at Lindenau. The Coalition armies stood round them.</li><li><b>The Coalition attacks from all sides</b> The Allies attacked from every side and completely encircled the French army. After over nine hours of fighting the French were slowly forced back towards Leipzig.</li><li><b>Bertrand drives the Austrians from Lindenau</b> On the western side the French IV Corps drove the Austrian III Corps away from Lindenau. This broke the encirclement and cleared the way for the French retreat.</li><li><b>The French withdraw in the night</b> The French army was ordered to withdraw silently through Leipzig and over its single bridge across the Elster. A rearguard was left to hold the city for a day or a little longer, while the Allies did not realise that the army was leaving.</li><li><b>The Allies storm Leipzig</b> The Allies learned of the evacuation at 7 am and attacked from the north, south and east. Oudinot's rearguard held them up in street fighting among barricades and houses.</li><li><b>The bridge is blown and the rearguard is cut off</b> A corporal fired the fuses of the only bridge over the Elster while it was crowded with French troops and Oudinot's rearguard was still in Leipzig. The panic became a rout in which thousands died and 30,000 were captured. The rest of the army had crossed.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleon.jpg" alt="Napoleon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacques-Louis_David_-_The_Emperor_Napoleon_in_His_Study_at_the_Tuileries_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jacques-Louis David, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> on 24 June 1812 with around 685,000 troops and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in late 1812 after the bloody yet indecisive Battle of Borodino. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> refused to surrender, and the campaign ended in disaster as the French retreated through the Russian winter, with sickness, starvation and the harrying of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> and partisans. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grande-armee">Grande Armée</a> was virtually destroyed by the time it returned. In June 1813 the combined armies of Portugal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the United Kingdom under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a> routed the French at Vitoria. The Sixth Coalition formed, comprising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, Russia, Spain, Portugal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, the United Kingdom and smaller German states whose citizens and leaders were no longer loyal to Napoleon.</p><p>Napoleon raised another large army, mostly raw and young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscripts</a>, and sought to regain the offensive in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. He won hard-fought tactical victories at Lützen on 2 May and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bautzen">Bautzen</a> on 20–21 May, and a brief armistice followed. During it the monarchs of Russia and Prussia met Charles John of Sweden at Trachenberg Castle, where a strategy was outlined that became known as the Trachenberg Plan once <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> joined the Coalition on 12 August 1813. The Coalition armies were to avoid battle with Napoleon himself, retreat whenever he advanced and target the forces of his marshals. The Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> engaged him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> on 27 August and the French won, but close adherence to the plan brought Coalition victories at Großbeeren, Kulm, Katzbach and Dennewitz.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Leipzig&#8221; cites where it covers Background, Prelude:</p><ul><li>David G. Chandler, The Campaigns of Napoleon (The Macmillan Company, 1966)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/karl-philipp-prince-of-schwarzenberg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karl-philipp-prince-of-schwarzenberg.jpg" alt="Karl Philipp, Prince of Schwarzenberg" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/karl-philipp-prince-of-schwarzenberg">Karl Philipp, Prince of Schwarzenberg</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karel_Filip_Schwarzenberg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unidentified painter, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The French had around 160,000 soldiers and 700 guns, plus 10,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, 9,000 Italians and 19,000 Germans of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a>, a total of 198,000 troops. By other estimates their combined forces were up to 225,000, and the guns brought in over all four days numbered 900. The Coalition had around 380,000 troops and 1,500 guns: 145,000–150,000 Russians, 115,000 Austrians, 75,000–90,000 Prussians and from 19,000 to 23,000–30,000 Swedes. Most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s troops were teenagers and inexperienced men conscripted after the near destruction of the army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and his cavalry was insufficient, which made it hard to scout enemy positions or watch his lines of communication.</p><p>The Coalition had four army-level commands: the Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-philipp-prince-of-schwarzenberg">Schwarzenberg</a>, the Army of Silesia under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gebhard-leberecht-von-blucher">Blücher</a>, the Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> under Bennigsen, and the Army of the North, of Prussian, Russian and Swedish troops, under Charles John. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a>, Frederick William III and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Francis I</a> were present. Napoleon planned to take the offensive between the Pleiße and the Parthe. The converging rivers split the ground into four sectors, and by holding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> and its bridges he could shift troops between them faster than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> could. Ney and Marmont defended the north, MacDonald the east, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozef-poniatowski">Poniatowski</a> and Augereau the Wachau and Liebertwolkwitz flank. Drouot was to command the main battery on the Gallows Height.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Leipzig&#8221; cites where it covers Opposing forces, French plans, Coalition plans:</p><ul><li>David G. Chandler, The Campaigns of Napoleon (The Macmillan Company, 1966)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gebhard-leberecht-von-blucher"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gebhard-leberecht-von-blucher.jpg" alt="Gebhard Leberecht von Blücher" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gebhard-leberecht-von-blucher">Gebhard Leberecht von Blücher</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Field_Marshal_Prince_von_Blucher.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">George Dawe, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On 16 October the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> attacked at Dölitz, Markkleeberg, Wachau and Liebertwolkwitz in the south. At Liebertwolkwitz <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> had Drouot form a grand battery of 150 guns and sent Murat in with 10,000 cavalry, but Murat's massed columns were harried by smaller Allied cavalry formations and driven back. Napoleon gained ground but could not break the Allied lines. In the north the Battle of Möckern, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gebhard-leberecht-von-blucher">Blücher</a>'s Russian and Prussian corps fought Marmont's VI Corps, lasted well into the night, with 9,000 Allied and 7,000 French casualties and another 2,000 French prisoners. The first day ended in a draw. On 17 October there were only two actions, and the Allies received 145,000 troops against the French 14,000.</p><p>On 18 October Napoleon sent the captured Merveldt back on parole with an offer to give up the fortresses on the Oder and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> if he could withdraw behind the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a>, and to sign an armistice. The three monarchs declined. The Allies then attacked from all sides, encircling the French and forcing them back towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>. The bloodiest fighting was at Probstheida, where garden walls, artillery and the Imperial Guard repelled repeated Prussian and Russian assaults. 5,400 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> of Reynier's VII Corps defected to Charles John's army, and Württemberg cavalry also deserted. The French artillery had only 20,000 rounds left, and that night Napoleon began withdrawing across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-elster">White Elster</a>.</p><p>The Allies learned of the evacuation at 7:00 on 19 October and attacked between 8:00 and 9:00, but Oudinot's 30,000 men fought street by street in Leipzig. At 1:00 in the afternoon the bridge over the Elster was blown while it was crowded, causing a rout.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Leipzig&#8221; cites where it covers 16 October, 17 October, 18 October, 19 October:</p><ul><li>David G. Chandler, The Campaigns of Napoleon (The Macmillan Company, 1966)</li><li>Antony Brett-James, Europe Against Napoleon: The Leipzig Campaign, 1813, from Eyewitness Accounts (Macmillan, 1970)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Estimates of the total killed, wounded or missing range from 80,000 to 110,000. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> lost about 38,000 killed and wounded. In the retreat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> captured 15,000 able-bodied Frenchmen, 21,000 wounded or sick, 325 guns, 28 eagles, standards or colours and most of the French supply trains. Six French generals were killed, 12 wounded and 36 captured, including Lauriston and Reynier. Of a total Allied force of 360,000, approximately 54,000 were casualties: 34,000 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-philipp-prince-of-schwarzenberg">Schwarzenberg</a>'s Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, 12,000 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gebhard-leberecht-von-blucher">Blücher</a>'s Army of Silesia and about 4,000 each in the armies of Charles John and Bennigsen. Locals had difficulty disposing of the corpses, and some were still visible the following year.</p><p>The Coalition's heavy losses and exhaustion made prompt pursuit impossible, and the French retreated at a fast pace towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. The Bavarian army tried to block them at Hanau, where the French won a minor victory. The battle ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a>'s presence east of the Rhine and brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and Württemberg over to the Coalition. Napoleon is quoted as telling the Senate at the year's end, "A year ago all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> marched with us; today all Europe marches against us." The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a> was dissolved and the Coalition invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in early 1814. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> fell on 31 March, Napoleon abdicated on 6 April and he reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elba">Elba</a> on 30 May.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Leipzig&#8221; cites where it covers Casualties, Aftermath:</p><ul><li>David G. Chandler, The Campaigns of Napoleon (The Macmillan Company, 1966)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia.jpg" alt="Alexander I of Russia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I of Russia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_I_of_Russia_by_G.Dawe_(1826,_Peterhof)-crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">George Dawe, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Estimates of the numbers at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> differ. The French are put at 198,000 troops, or up to 225,000 by other estimates, and the Coalition at around 380,000 or 360,000. One estimate puts the whole battle at about 560,000 soldiers and 133,000 casualties, another at 80,000 to 110,000 killed, wounded or missing. The first day's losses were approximately 30,000 for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> and about 25,000 for the French.</p><p>Several points rest on reports of one person’s motives. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> is reported to have objected that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-philipp-prince-of-schwarzenberg">Schwarzenberg</a>'s first plan was disastrous because it would not let the Coalition encircle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, and later events are said to have proved him correct: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gebhard-leberecht-von-blucher">Blücher</a>'s attack in the north and the Russian Guard succeeded, while the Austrian action along the Pleiße failed. Charles John's slow advance on 18 October was purportedly due to news of a planned French attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>' defection is explained by their esteem for him from Wagram. The blowing of the bridge had been delegated from General Dulauloy to Colonel Montfort to a corporal who did not know the planned time schedule.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars.jpg" alt="Napoleonic Wars" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Napoleonic_Wars_(revision).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ruedi33a, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, also known as the Battle of the Nations, was the largest battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>, surpassing Borodino, Wagram, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a> and Auerstedt, Ulm and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>, and as the bloodiest, with over 400,000 rounds of artillery ammunition expended. It is called arguably the deciding battle of the wars. It was the second French army <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> had lost in as many years, and it severely damaged his reputation of invincibility. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> became one of the continent's great powers again after its severe setbacks in 1806.</p><p>In 1863, exactly 50 years after the battle, a monument to the blasting of the bridge was inaugurated in Leipzig. The 91-metre Monument to the Battle of the Nations (Völkerschlachtdenkmal), designed by Bruno Schmitz, was completed in 1913 at a cost of six million German gold marks. The Russian Memorial Church was dedicated to coincide with the hundredth anniversary and honours the Russian troops. The course of the fighting in the city is marked by numerous monuments and by 50 Apel-stones that mark important lines of the French and Allied troops. The Centennial Exhibition of 1913 was held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Breslau</a>, now Wrocław, where its Centennial Hall was listed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2006.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>From 16 to 19 October 1813 the Sixth Coalition's armies defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grande-armee">Grande Armée</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, in the largest battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a> was dissolved, and the Coalition invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in early 1814.</em></p>

